WEBVTT

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.120
User: Hey Reachy, I'm trying to focus.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.960
Reachy: I'm sorry, I'll stop the vacuuming right away.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:23.032
People don't just talk. They look, gesture, and touch.

00:00:23.882 --> 00:00:28.635
Our framework gives a robot all three: touch, vision, and speech.

00:00:29.485 --> 00:00:32.325
A vision-language model reasons over them together,

00:00:32.325 --> 00:00:34.848
and responds with coordinated motion and speech.

00:00:35.698 --> 00:00:40.098
Speech and touch are converted to text, and either one triggers a response.

00:00:40.948 --> 00:00:43.541
The robot's eye camera adds visual context.

00:00:44.391 --> 00:00:47.349
An interaction history carries context across turns.

00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:51.559
Gemini 3 Flash returns a list of action and speech pairs,

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:54.348
which the robot performs while speaking in a cloned voice.

00:00:55.198 --> 00:00:57.918
New input can interrupt the robot at any time:

00:00:57.918 --> 00:01:00.558
the model decides whether to stop the current motion,

00:01:00.558 --> 00:01:03.180
and the robot stops talking when the user speaks.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.800
Each virtual hand is covered with sphere colliders,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.919
and the physics engine reports where, and how hard, they touch the robot.

00:01:12.769 --> 00:01:15.429
A classifier recognizes eight social touch gestures,

00:01:15.429 --> 00:01:19.240
from pokes and taps to strokes and punches.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:24.506
The VLM receives it as text: the human pressed your left shoulder once.

00:01:25.356 --> 00:01:29.686
Eighteen participants wore a Quest 3 headset and custom haptic gloves.

00:01:30.536 --> 00:01:36.796
They completed five scenarios: asking for a joke, making the robot move, cheering it up,

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:39.816
getting its attention while it vacuums, and asking for a drink.

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:46.523
Each ran twice: with our system, and with a human teleoperator as an upper baseline.

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:51.411
After each one, they rated naturalness and appropriateness in the headset.

00:01:56.523 --> 00:01:59.183
User: Hey Reachy, can you pass me the water bottle?

00:02:00.593 --> 00:02:02.273
Reachy: Sure thing, one water coming right up.

00:02:07.773 --> 00:02:09.173
Reachy: Here you go, stay hydrated.

00:02:12.518 --> 00:02:14.578
User: Hey, Reachy. I need to go to the bathroom.

00:02:17.038 --> 00:02:18.338
Reachy: Oh, sure thing.

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:19.485
Reachy: I'll step aside so you can pass.

00:02:19.785 --> 00:02:24.125
Our system was rated above neutral on appropriateness and naturalness,

00:02:24.125 --> 00:02:27.865
though the teleoperator scored higher, especially on naturalness.

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:31.695
Most breakdowns were about conversational timing,

00:02:31.695 --> 00:02:34.951
with responses taking 3.7 seconds on average.

00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:40.041
Touch accounted for a larger share of interactions with our system.

00:02:40.041 --> 00:02:43.800
Whether it was used to repair conversations remains an open question.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.798
Touch accounted for a larger share of interactions.

00:02:47.798 --> 00:02:51.130
Its role in conversational repair needs further study.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:56.450
Next, we will cut latency, add clarifying questions, and move to a physical robot.

